#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Per-demo NARRATIVE derivation check (K-1051).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. `kiosk-demo-*/before-after.md` is a PUBLISHED per-demo
# surface — four documents, ~1780 lines, quoting recorded transcripts and
# shipped source at length — and until this script nothing compared a single
# byte of it against anything. bin/check-demo-copies:SKELETON_NOT_COMPARED names
# the file only to EXCLUDE it («per-demo narrative: that storefront's
# before/after contrast»), and unlike the README entry beside it, that exclusion
# carried no pointer to a checker of its own. bin/check-ci-tasks never mentions
# it, and neither does the gate that guards hand-kept file inventories.
#
# The cost was measured, not assumed. Ten ledger rows live in these four files
# (K-1053..K-1057, K-1062..K-1066, and K-774/K-778/K-812/K-817/K-818/K-832/K-1036
# before them) and EVERY ONE was found by a human eye reading the documents in
# one sitting — never by a gate. K-1062 is the shape that matters: getgrocery
# and hoteling published transcript blocks under an explicit «verbatim … nothing
# else touched» guarantee that contained five and three assertion strings the
# tasks no longer print, omitted getgrocery's trailing `Server stopped.`, and in
# hoteling's case omitted an entire RUN the task prints.
#
# WHY IT IS A SEPARATE SCRIPT, AND NOT A SECTION OF bin/check-demo-copies
# (K-1096). That file's own header states the boundary it holds: bin/check-ci-tasks
# is a SIBLING rather than a mode of it «because the two answer different
# questions from different inputs: that one compares ci.yml against each demo's
# rake tasks, this one compares demo files against each other». The boundary is
# the comparison INPUT. This check's input is a THIRD pair — one demo's document
# against THAT SAME DEMO's own producers — so it is a third sibling by the very
# rule that kept the first two apart. (The cross-demo half of the narrative
# class, where the input IS demo-against-demo, is correctly in check-demo-copies
# already: the K-1069 framework-prose block.)
#
# ── THE THREE DESIGN FACTS THIS IS BUILT ON, each bought by a repair ──────────
#
# 1. A BLOCK MUST NAME ITS PRODUCER MACHINE-READABLY. The four documents named
#    theirs in prose, four different ways; prose parsing rots. So every fenced
#    block carries an HTML comment on the line immediately above it:
#
#      <!-- derived: transcript | task: bundle exec rake demo:book
#           | from: lib/tasks/demo.rake, script/book_flow.rb | abridged: none -->
#      <!-- derived: snippet | from: app/controllers/kiosk/x_controller.rb
#           | transform: strip_descriptions | abridged: two marked elisions -->
#      <!-- derived: generator
#           | from: kiosk-server/lib/generators/kiosk/install/install_generator.rb -->
#      <!-- derived: none | why: an illustrative one-line swap no file here carries -->
#
#    A fenced block with no declaration FAILS. That is the property that matters
#    most: a new block cannot appear silently, the same way a new duplicated path
#    cannot in bin/check-demo-copies.
#
# 2. THREE BLOCK CLASSES, THREE CHECKS — one check cannot serve them.
#    TRANSCRIPT: «every line is a line the named task prints».
#    SNIPPET:    «every line is a line of that file, modulo the declared
#                 transformation».
#    GENERATOR:  «every line invokes the namespace the named generator answers»,
#                 where that namespace is DERIVED twice — from the generator's
#                 path and from its class nesting — and the two must agree
#                 (K-1099, which found one invocation exempted as
#                 `derived: none` in three documents at once).
#
# 3. LINE MEMBERSHIP, NOT EQUALITY, AND NEVER REGENERATION. UUIDs, settlement
#    ids, `pi_…` references, ports and dates change every run, so a guard cannot
#    diff a block against a fresh run — and worse, getgrocery's transcript has
#    two legitimate shapes depending on the Dublin hour (the K-480 past-slot
#    control is a no-op after the last slot of the day), so a guard that
#    REGENERATES flaps where one checking membership does not. This check never
#    boots Rails, never touches a database and never runs a demo.
#
# ── HOW THE TRANSCRIPT CHECK WORKS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Ripper lexes each declared producer and every string literal in it becomes a
# LINE PATTERN: literal text with `#{…}` interpolations replaced by a wildcard,
# split on newlines, adjacent `"a" \ "b"` literals merged (that shape is how
# most long assertion messages are written, and treating the halves as two
# patterns would make the check useless on exactly the lines K-1062 was about).
#
# A transcript line is ACCOUNTED FOR when one of these holds:
#
#   (a) a pattern matches it whole;
#   (b) a pattern matches it whole as a FRAME and every wildcard it captured is
#       itself accounted for — empty, a number one of the producers writes as a
#       numeric literal, a whitespace-free word one of them can print, or a
#       pattern in its own right. This is what resolves `puts "  OK    #{msg}"`
#       against the `msg` literal two hundred lines away, without the frame's
#       `.*?` swallowing arbitrary prose;
#   (b') a frame with enough IDENTIFYING literal text of its own is matched
#       without reading its captures at all, because at that point the literal
#       is doing the work — but even there, no capture may swallow the literal
#       that terminates it. What counts as identifying, and what a capture is
#       allowed to be, is the K-1105 section below.
#   (c) it is a JSON object — the driver's summary line — and every key at every
#       depth is a name some producer names. This branch is EXCLUSIVE: a line
#       that parses as a JSON object is checked here and nowhere else, because
#       when it fell through to (a)/(b) first a thin frame elsewhere in the
#       producer matched the whole line and a planted key rename went green.
#       Its NESTED keys are the handler's,
#       not the driver's (the driver splices a response object in whole), so a
#       transcript may name the handler in `keys_from:`. That is a SEPARATE list
#       on purpose: those files are consulted for key names only and their
#       literals never join the line-pattern set, because a controller is large
#       and folding it in would blunt the line check this exists for.
#
# Anything else is an error quoting the line. A doc line carrying a message the
# task no longer prints is exactly case (b) failing, which is all five stale
# getgrocery strings and all three hoteling ones.
#
# ── HOW THE SNIPPET CHECK WORKS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every line must be a member of the UNION of the source's raw lines and the
# lines of each DECLARED transformation of that source. The transformations are
# EXECUTABLE, not prose:
#
#   strip_descriptions — deletes each verb's prose `description` call (leaving
#                        the marker line the documents already use) and every
#                        `description:` key inside a schema, reattaching what
#                        followed it. This is the transformation the four
#                        documents describe in prose; here it is a function.
#   dedent             — the source's own indentation is not required to
#                        survive quoting (config/routes.rb is quoted without the
#                        `routes.draw do` indent). Compares lstripped.
#
# The document's OWN comments inside a snippet are permitted only through the
# marked-elision shape the repair wave introduced:
#
#   # ── <WHAT> ELIDED HERE (this comment is the document's, not the file's) ──
#
# followed by a run of comment lines. A document comment that is not introduced
# by that exact marker is an error, because it is indistinguishable from a line
# the reader will take as shipped code.
#
# ── AND THE PROSE-PROMISE RULE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ABRIDGEMENT MUST BE DECLARED IN A FORM THIS SCRIPT READS, NOT PROMISED IN
# PROSE. Every «nothing else touched» / «nothing removed and nothing added» /
# «unedited» / «verbatim» promise in these four documents was FALSE when K-1062
# and K-1063 read them — so the promise is exactly the sentence that must be
# BACKED by something mechanical rather than trusted. Three rules, and note what
# they deliberately are NOT:
#
#   * A promise in the window beside a block declared `derived: none` OR
#     `derived: transcript` is an ERROR. `none` is checked by nothing, so
#     dressing it as verbatim is the case where the prose is the ONLY guarantee.
#     `transcript` is checked by a SUBSET test, which is the same hole in a
#     different shape: membership backs «every line here is a line the task
#     prints» and cannot reach «first line to last», «the whole output» or «all
#     three runs». See the K-1106 section for why the second half of that
#     sentence was missing for as long as the rule existed.
#   * `abridged: none` and a `transform:`, or `abridged: none` and a marked
#     elision inside the block, are a CONTRADICTION between the declaration and
#     the block, and fail.
#   * A declared `transform:` that no line in the block needs is DECORATION and
#     fails — a transformation is declared because it was applied, and one
#     nobody applied is a promise again.
#
#   They are NOT a ban on the phrases. A qualified promise — «every line is that
#   file's with its `description` cut out and nothing else altered» — is what the
#   K-1062 repair wave deliberately wrote, and it is TRUE precisely because
#   `strip_descriptions` below makes it checkable. Banning the sentence would
#   delete the honest form along with the dishonest one; what this script does
#   instead is refuse to let the sentence stand alone.
#
# ── AND THE PROMISE RULE NOW COVERS THE CLASS IT WAS WRITTEN FOR (K-1106) ────
#
# The rule above shipped INSIDE the `if kind == "none"` branch, and K-1099 then
# took that branch down to a single user — hoteling's illustrative one-line
# swap. So a rule written because «verbatim … nothing else touched» had been
# FALSE eight times over fired beside exactly ONE block — that `derived: none`
# swap, which is the one declared block this script does NOT check — and beside
# none of the four that quote a recorded run, which is exactly where those eight
# falsehoods had been. (The split is printed, not remembered: `--list` prints
# every DECLARED block and the success line counts the CHECKED ones — 25 and 24
# when this was written, and both move with the next block anyone declares.) MEASURED at head before this change, by re-implementing
# the window and the regexes over the four documents: the transcript intros
# carried ELEVEN completeness promises, NINE matched a `PROMISES` regex, and
# only SEVEN fell inside the window.
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT HOLES, BOTH CLOSED HERE.
#
#   1. THE CLASS. `transcript` now fails on a promise exactly as `none` does.
#      A transcript check is set membership and no amount of membership backs a
#      completeness claim — K-1062's hoteling block was missing an ENTIRE RUN
#      the task prints, and every line it did quote was genuine, so the check
#      that exists would have passed it. The four intros were rewritten into the
#      K-1062 repair wave's qualified shape: each says what the mechanism does
#      hold (every line is one a declared producer prints, and a line they
#      cannot print reddens the tree) and hands completeness back to `abridged:`,
#      naming it there as a human's signature.
#   2. THE WINDOW. getgrocery scored ZERO promise hits, and not because it made
#      no promise: «unedited, first line to last» sat TWO paragraphs above its
#      declaration, behind «**This recording is the secret-free path**», and
#      `paragraph_before` takes one. `promise_window_before` takes the whole
#      INTRO instead — see its own comment for why widening is safe for THIS
#      rule and would not be for a rule that reads the prose as a description.
#
# ── THE PRICE THE WIDE WINDOW CAME WITH, AND WHERE IT IS PAID (T-129) ───────
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A KIND TO `PROMISE_KINDS`. It used to live only beside
# `promise_window_boundary?`, twelve hundred lines down, which is a constraint
# the next author meets when it fires rather than when they need it.
#
# The window runs from the nearest preceding heading, `---` rule or fence down
# to the declaration. Where two blocks share a section with no heading between
# them, the nearest fence above is the EARLIER block's closing one, so the
# window spans that block's own trailing commentary and attributes its sentences
# here. MEASURED at head with the shipped neighbourhood, over all 25 declared
# blocks: unfloored, the promise vocabulary hits 3 `generator` blocks and 13
# `snippet` blocks; every one of the three generator hits is a `verbatim`
# inherited from a preceding Gemfile or routes commentary. The rule covers
# neither kind, so it cost nothing — which is what T-129 recorded, and the row's
# reading of WHY is corrected here rather than carried: it said the four
# `transcript` blocks and the one `none` block inherit nothing. The transcripts
# do not (each is the first declared block under a heading). Hoteling's `none`
# block DOES — it sits directly under a `snippet` — and it is green only because
# those eight lines happen to contain no promise word, in a place where
# «nothing else altered» is the qualified sentence the K-1062 repair wave
# deliberately wrote. So the false positive was one sentence away, not one
# extension away.
#
# THE FLOOR closes it: the window starts after the previous block's
# after-paragraph, unless that paragraph is ALSO this block's own
# `paragraph_before` — the gap holding exactly one paragraph, which serves both
# and which hoteling's `none` block is the corpus's only instance of. MEASURED
# after the floor: 0 generator hits, 13 snippet hits, transcripts and `none`
# unchanged at 0. Both directions are fixtures in `--self-test` rather than
# prose, because the corpus has one instance of this geometry and a document
# rewrite would take the proof with it.
#
# WHAT WAS NOT DONE, AND WHY. The rule was NOT extended to `snippet`, where the
# other thirteen promise hits live. Those are the weaker case and sweeping them
# in unread would be the over-claim this section is about: a snippet's «every
# line is that file's with its `description` cut out and nothing else altered»
# is largely BACKED, because membership gives the first clause and the declared
# `strip_descriptions` transform makes the second checkable. That qualified form
# is what the repair wave deliberately wrote, and banning the phrase would
# delete the honest sentence along with the dishonest one.
#
# ── AND `abridged:` IS BOUND TO THE RAKE DAG AS FAR AS IT GOES (K-1101) ──────
#
# `abridged:` was REQUIRED and never READ: any non-empty phrase satisfied it. So
# hoteling and getgrocery both declared — and said again in the prose — that the
# cut above their first quoted line was «demo:setup's db:drop/db:create/
# db:schema:load/db:seed chatter and nothing else». Measured by running the task:
# ELEVEN lines for hoteling, of which FIVE are not db chatter (rake's echo of the
# two `psql` commands that create and grant `app_role`, the `NOTICE`/`LOCATION`
# pair Postgres answers the grant with, and `[kiosk] WARNING: generated an
# EPHEMERAL signing key`), and THIRTY-THREE for getgrocery, which adds a
# `[getgrocery] no STRIPE_SECRET_KEY…` line to the same five. The BOUNDARY claim
# was exact in both. Only the enumeration was wrong — and a human reading the
# document is what found it, which is the same sentence the top of this file
# opens with.
#
# THE SPLIT THIS DRAWS, AND WHY. What the cut CONTAINS cannot be checked here at
# any price: it requires running the task, which design fact 3 refuses (ids and
# dates move every run, and getgrocery's transcript has a legitimate wall-clock
# branch). What the cut IS — the output of a task this demo defines and that the
# quoted task depends on — is a fact about the rake DAG, which is static. So the
# declaration stops enumerating the lines and NAMES THE TASK, and the naming is
# held to three things:
#
#   * the cited task must be DEFINED in this demo's lib/tasks/*.rake, by the same
#     two spellings the `task:` field is checked with. Rename it and this fails.
#   * it must be a declared PREREQUISITE of the quoted `task:`. If the quoted task
#     never runs it, its output was never in that task's output and calling it an
#     abridgement is a category error — which is exactly why atablefor, whose
#     document quotes `demo:book` and prepares the database with a SEPARATE
#     `rake demo:setup`, correctly declares `abridged: none`.
#   * and no line of the BLOCK may be one that cited task's own body prints. A
#     declaration that a task's output was cut, over a block containing it, is the
#     same contradiction as `abridged: none` beside a marked elision. Note how
#     narrow this is: `demo:setup`'s repo-produced output is its three `sh "…"`
#     echoes and nothing else, because `Dropped database`, the psql `NOTICE` and
#     the seed listing come from Postgres, Rails and db/seeds.rb. It catches a
#     paste, not a paraphrase.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT CHECKED, and it is the larger half: that the cut is COMPLETE,
# that it contains what the prose says, and that nothing was quietly deleted from
# the MIDDLE of a block. `abridged:` remains a human's signature, and the
# document's own prose — where the reader-useful detail belongs — remains
# unbacked. What changed is only that the signature now names something this
# script can resolve instead of asserting a content list nothing could.
#
# ── AND `abridged:` NAMES THE CUT, OR NAMES THE KEPT SET **AND ITS UNIVERSE**
#    (K-1104) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This script's own error text used to tell an author the field is «`none`, or
# a phrase naming what was LEFT OUT», and the corpus it guards did not agree
# with it: of 21 declarations, six are cut-side, eleven are kept-side («X only,
# out of Y», «X only; Y are out») and four are transcripts. The majority form
# was the one the guidance did not describe, so an author following the message
# wrote one shape and an author following the documents wrote the other.
#
# Nothing here was FALSE — a kept-side declaration with an explicit universe
# determines the complement exactly as well as a cut-side one does — so the
# ruling picks the sense by the field's PURPOSE rather than by the count. The
# cut is what the reader cannot see; describing it is the job. The kept sense
# is therefore admissible, but only WITH the universe, because that is the
# thing that makes the complement derivable. The eleven kept-side declarations
# already satisfy that and were not rewritten.
#
# `KEPT_SIDE_RE` / `UNIVERSE_RE` carry as much of that as a script can: a
# declaration that has said «only» has committed to the kept sense out loud
# and must then name what it was only-of. Its limits are in the over-claim
# list, and they are real — a kept-side declaration that simply avoids the
# word escapes the check entirely, which is exactly what skooti's transcript
# did (K-1103 instance 1).
#
# ── AND A NUMBERED CUT LIST IS HELD TO THE FIELD BESIDE IT (K-1107) ─────────
#
# `abridged:` is the half of the pair this script reads; the paragraph three
# lines above it is the half a READER reads. Nothing tied the two together, and
# in two of K-1103's five instances the PARAGRAPH WAS RIGHT WHILE THE FIELD WAS
# WRONG — the worse direction of the two, because the field is the half with a
# checker. So: when that paragraph enumerates the cut as NUMBERED sentences
# («Three things were deleted. (1) … (2) … (3) …»), every backticked BARE
# IDENTIFIER inside those numbered sentences must appear in the field.
#
# MEASURED against the pre-K-1103 tree, extracted with `git archive fca7c1d1^`
# into a scratch directory so no working tree was touched: the rule flags
# hoteling's declaration on `search_hotels`, `hotel_detail` and `availability`
# — i.e. it catches K-1103 instance (2) mechanically, where a human found it.
# Run at head it has exactly ONE live hit, hoteling's `availability` again,
# which is a vagueness rather than a falsehood («one verb's guards at a marked
# line» where the prose names the verb) and is repaired in the same wave.
#
# SENTENCE-SCOPED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN — measured, not argued. The
# naive whole-paragraph form false-positives on THREE of the corpus's five
# enumerating paragraphs, because each of the three ends in a KEPT clause —
# «Everything else — field names, types, `required` lists, `enum`s …» — whose
# backticks name what STAYED. Reading a kept list as a cut list is how a check
# like this gets switched off, so the items are scoped to their `(N)` sentence
# and the paragraph's other backticks are ignored on purpose.
#
# IT IS A NAME-OVERLAP CHECK AND NOTHING MORE. Its limits are in the over-claim
# list and they are real: it cannot tell whether either half is right, it is
# blind to any cut the prose does not backtick, and it says nothing about the
# corpus's OTHER enumerating shape — the un-numbered «N of M shipped actions …
# are left out», which is what K-1103 instance (3) was.
#
# ── AND A FRAME'S LITERAL MUST IDENTIFY THE LINE, NOT MERELY EXIST (K-1105) ──
#
# A frame with enough literal text of its own is matched WITHOUT reading its
# wildcards — that shortcut is what lets `Properties: #{n} found, using
# property_id=#{id} (#{name})` account for a line carrying a hotel name this
# script cannot possibly predict. The bar was four non-whitespace literal
# characters, and `puts "  OK  #{label} == #{expected}"` clears it with `OK`
# and `==`. That frame prints TWENTY of the twenty-six published
# `  OK  … == …` verdict lines — the lines that say whether the payment, the
# booking and the unlock worked — so for as long as this script has existed
# those twenty were held to their punctuation and nothing else. MEASURED on a
# published line, not on a plant: hoteling's `  OK  http_pay == 200` mutated
# to `  OK  http_pay == 500 (charged nothing, refunded twice)` exited 0, and
# this script printed «every line held to a literal its declared producer
# prints» while it did. PRE-EXISTING and proven so: the same mutation was run
# against the guard AND the documents as they stood at e8606563, extracted
# with `git archive` into a scratch directory, and passed there too.
#
# THREE CHANGES, EACH THE SMALLEST ONE ITS OBSERVATION SUPPORTS.
#
#   * The bar counts ALPHANUMERICS. Punctuation is the scaffolding every line
#     of a shape shares; it cannot identify WHICH line this is. The number 4
#     is untouched, because the metric was the defect. `  OK  <W> == <W>` is
#     an anchor of 2 under it and falls to the checked path.
#   * A capture that is a BARE NUMBER must be a number one of the declared
#     producers writes as a numeric literal. This is the only rule here that
#     reaches a VALUE, and it is why `== 500` now fails where `== 200` passes.
#   * Any other whitespace-free capture must be a word one of them can PRINT
#     (string-literal text, symbol names, numerals — not identifiers, not
#     comments). This is a vocabulary check, not a truth check, and it is what
#     refuses an invented `http_paaaay` or a `confirm_status == cancelled`.
#
# And one repair to the unchecked path itself: a lazy `.*?` still backtracks
# over a literal it was meant to stop at, so a frame ending in a wildcard
# matched its own published line with prose appended after the closing
# bracket. `no_capture_swallows_its_terminator?` refuses that.
#
# THE PROOF IS THE PUBLISHED CORPUS, NOT A SAMPLE. Every one of the 26 lines
# was mutated in place, one at a time, in both a value form and a prose form
# (50 mutations); before these changes 47 went red and 3 stayed green, after
# them 50 go red, and the tree is restored from file copies and green at exit
# 0 either side. What still gets through is the swap bullet in the list above,
# and it is a smaller hole of a class this script already declared.
#
# AND THE PRICE, WHICH THIS SECTION DID NOT STATE UNTIL K-1108. Narrowing a
# guard buys precision with false POSITIVES, and these three rules have one:
# they can redden a line that is HONEST. A demo that prints a COMPUTED number —
# a total, a duration, an elapsed count that no declared producer writes as an
# `:on_int`/`:on_float` literal — fails whenever the frame around it carries
# fewer than four ALPHANUMERIC characters, which `  OK  #{label} == #{value}`
# does. Today's corpus pays nothing for that (50 of 50 mutations red, baseline
# exit 0); the exposure is the NEXT demo. The answer there is to give the frame
# literal text that identifies the line, or to name the value in a producer —
# never to put the bar back on punctuation, which is the metric measured broken.
#
# ── WHAT THIS DOES **NOT** COVER. Stated plainly, because an over-claimed guard
# is the defect this class keeps producing and K-1069's block was accepted
# precisely for declaring its own holes. ────────────────────────────────────
#
#   * IT CANNOT PROVE A TRANSCRIPT IS COMPLETE. Membership is a SUBSET test. A
#     block that silently drops an entire RUN the task prints — which is half of
#     K-1062 — still passes. Completeness needs a real run, which is what
#     `abridged:` declares instead of proves, and `abridged: none` is therefore
#     a HUMAN's assertion this script only keeps consistent with the prose.
#     K-1101 narrowed this and did not close it: a task NAMED in `abridged:` is
#     now resolved against the rake DAG and held off the block, so the field can
#     no longer cite something that does not exist or is never run. What the cut
#     CONTAINS is still the human's word.
#   * IT CANNOT PROVE THE LINES ARE IN THE RIGHT ORDER, or that they came from
#     ONE run. Two lines from mutually exclusive branches both pass.
#   * IT CANNOT SEE A LINE PRINTED BY A PRODUCER NOBODY DECLARED. `from:` is a
#     human's list; a line that came from an undeclared file simply fails, and a
#     wrong line that happens to match a declared file's literal simply passes.
#   * AND SINCE K-1105 THAT SAME HOLE EXISTS ONE SIZE SMALLER, AT THE TOKEN.
#     The capture rules are VOCABULARIES, not value sets: they refuse a number
#     or a word the producers cannot say, and accept any that they can. So a
#     SWAP between two of the producer's own tokens passes — measured at head,
#     all four of `  OK  http_pay == 200` → `== 201`, → `== 403`,
#     `http_pay` → `http_availability`, and getgrocery's
#     `slots_badzone_code == bad_request` → `== not_found` exit 0. Reaching
#     those needs the ARGUMENTS of the call that printed the line, not the
#     file's vocabulary, and that is dataflow this script does not do.
#   * A LINE WITH NO LITERAL TEXT AT ALL — `puts x` — is invisible to the
#     transcript check unless it is JSON. Its content is unconstrained.
#   * THE JSON RULE CHECKS KEYS, NEVER VALUES. `"http_pay":200` and
#     `"http_pay":500` are equally acceptable here. Note the asymmetry K-1105
#     leaves behind: the plain assertion line `  OK  http_pay == 500` now
#     FAILS, while the same claim inside the JSON summary line still passes.
#     Values there are unconstrained because a summary line's values are the
#     run's own — ids, timestamps, amounts — and nothing static bounds them.
#   * `derived: none` IS AN UNCHECKED ESCAPE HATCH. It demands a `why:` and
#     nothing more, exactly as `:per_demo` does in bin/check-demo-copies. K-1099
#     took three of its four users away — the `rails g kiosk:install` fence,
#     which is now `derived: generator` — leaving ONE: hoteling's illustrative
#     one-line swap, which genuinely has no producer in this repository.
#   * `derived: generator` CHECKS THE NAMESPACE, NOT THE EFFECT. It proves that
#     an adopter typing this line reaches the generator the block names; it says
#     nothing about what that generator then emits. The prose beside the fence
#     («this emits exactly two things …») is K-1069's territory, in
#     bin/check-demo-copies, which binds it to the generator's own source.
#   * THE SNIPPET CHECK IS SET MEMBERSHIP, so it cannot see a reordering, a
#     duplication, or a deletion — only a line that is in no version of the file.
#   * THE `abridged:` UNIVERSE RULE IS A SHAPE CHECK. It fires on the word
#     «only» and asks for an «out of …» / «… are out» clause beside it; it
#     cannot tell whether the universe named is the right one, cannot tell
#     whether the kept set named is what the block actually quotes, and — the
#     one that matters most — does not fire at all on a kept-side declaration
#     that avoids the word. Every instance K-1103 recorded was found by a human
#     reading the document against the file, which is how every defect in this
#     class has ever been found.
#   * IT DOES NOT ADJUDICATE WHETHER A COMPLETENESS PROMISE IS TRUE. Membership
#     backs «every line quoted here is a real line», never «no line is missing»,
#     so a document may still promise more than this script can see — which is
#     why `abridged:` is a DECLARATION a human signs, not a proof. Since K-1106
#     it does REFUSE the promise beside the two block classes nothing can back
#     one for (`none`, `transcript`), and that is a phrase ban, not a truth
#     test: it says such a sentence may not be published there, never that the
#     sentence was false.
#   * THE PROMISE WINDOW IS A GEOMETRY, NOT AN ATTRIBUTION (T-129). Where the
#     gap between two blocks holds ONE paragraph it is read as belonging to
#     both, because a reader cannot tell either; where it holds more, the first
#     belongs to the block above and the last to the block below, and any
#     paragraph BETWEEN them belongs to neither and is invisible to this rule.
#     That middle ground is empty in the four documents today and is the price
#     of not attributing one block's sentence to another.
#   * AND `PROMISES` IS A VOCABULARY, NOT A CLASSIFIER. Eight regexes, every one
#     of them added because a document in this corpus said that exact thing —
#     two of them (K-1106) only after a re-measurement found «all three runs»,
#     the strongest completeness claim in these four files, matching nothing.
#     A promise phrased in words nobody has used yet passes, and the rule is
#     therefore a ratchet on known shapes rather than a bound on the class.
#   * THE CUT-ENUMERATION RULE (K-1107) IS NAME OVERLAP, NOT TRUTH. It fires
#     only where the paragraph before a declaration enumerates the cut in TWO OR
#     MORE numbered sentences, and asks only that each backticked bare
#     identifier in them also appear in `abridged:`. It cannot tell whether the
#     prose is right, whether the field is right, or whether they describe the
#     same thing in different words; it is blind to any cut the prose does not
#     backtick; and it does not fire at all on the corpus's un-numbered shape
#     («N of M shipped actions … are left out»), which two declarations use.
#   * IT SAYS NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THE PROSE. Whether the paragraphs around a block
#     are true is K-1069's question (cross-demo agreement, in check-demo-copies)
#     and the claims-versus-behaviour audit's.
#
# EACH CLASS ABOVE WAS PROVEN BY A PLANT — break it, watch this go red, put it
# back from a file copy. Twelve of them, all exit 1 and all restored to exit 0:
# a block with its declaration deleted; a transcript line carrying a word the
# task does not print; a renamed top-level and a renamed NESTED JSON key; a
# snippet line whose value was changed; a document comment with no elision
# marker; `abridged: none` on a block that has one; a `transform:` no line
# needs; a `from:` naming a file that moved; a `task:` naming a rake task that
# does not exist; a completeness promise beside a `derived: none` block; and a
# demo dropped from DOCUMENTED_DEMOS while it still ships the document. The
# third of those is why (c) is exclusive: it PASSED before that ordering fix.
# K-1099 added five more, same discipline: a generator fence drifted to
# `rails g kiosk:setup` (fails in that document alone); the generator's own
# directory renamed (fails in all three at once, which is the property the
# `derived: none` hatch did not have); the generator's class nesting renamed
# while its path stayed (path and class then derive different namespaces); a
# demo dropped from GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS while it still declares the block;
# and one demo's block reverted to the `derived: none` hatch, which is the
# «silently drops the recipe» direction of the same corpus rule.
#
# K-1101 added four, same discipline: an `abridged:` citing a task no rake file
# in the demo defines (which is what BOTH false declarations became the moment
# they were read — `db:drop` is Rails', not the demo's); one citing a real task
# of the demo that the quoted `task:` does not depend on; one citing a task the
# quoted task has no prerequisites at all; and a block with two lines of the
# cited task's own output pasted back into it. And one PROOF-OF-FIRE rather than
# a plant: restoring either document's pre-K-1101 declaration verbatim reddens
# this script.
#
# K-1105 replaced the plant with the sharper instrument the class had been
# missing: a MUTATION of a line already published. All 26 `  OK  … == …`
# verdict lines in the corpus (getgrocery 11, hoteling 9, skooti 6) were
# mutated in place one at a time, each in a value form (a number, or the
# verdict word, swapped for one the producers never name) and a prose form
# (« (charged nothing, refunded twice)» appended) — 50 mutations, each
# restored from a file copy before the next. BEFORE: 47 red, 3 green — two
# verdict-word swaps and one appended-prose line. AFTER: 50 red, with the
# tree green at exit 0 either side of the run. The three plants the ledger
# row was filed with are red too (`  OK  http_register == 418 teapot`,
# `  OK  totally_made_up_verb == 999 utterly invented prose`, and the
# published-line mutation `  OK  http_pay == 500 (charged nothing, refunded
# twice)`), and so is the bare `  OK  http_pay == 500`, which is the one that
# reaches a value.
#
# K-1106 added four plants and a standing control, all restored from file
# copies, tree green at exit 0 either side. Each of the three re-planted
# promises is a sentence this corpus PUBLISHED until this wave: getgrocery's
# «unedited, first line to last» put back THREE paragraphs above its declaration
# (red — which is the window fix proving itself, since the old window saw
# nothing two paragraphs out); hoteling's «all three runs» (red, quoting the
# phrase, which matched no regex before this wave); atablefor's «none of its
# chatter is in here» (red, same). Fourth, the rule the hoist could have broken:
# a `verbatim` beside hoteling's `derived: none` block still fails with the
# `derived: none` wording. The standing control is the corpus itself — FIVE
# snippet blocks carry «nothing else altered» and the tree is green, because the
# rule deliberately does not sweep the class whose promise is largely backed.
#
# K-1107 added two plants and two controls. PROOF-OF-FIRE: hoteling's pre-fix
# `abridged:` restored verbatim reddens, naming `availability`. A plant: drop
# `kyc_status` from getgrocery's field and it reddens naming it. The control
# pair is what proves the scoping rather than the rule — a new backticked
# `planted_kept_name` added to the KEPT clause («Everything else — field names,
# types, `required` lists …») stays GREEN, and the SAME name moved into
# numbered item (2) reddens. And a declared-limit control: getgrocery's
# un-numbered «Two of getgrocery's four shipped actions, `payment_setup` and
# `request_kyc`, are left out» does NOT fire when the field stops naming them,
# which is the over-claim list's own bullet demonstrated rather than asserted.
#
# K-1108 is not a plant but a READING, which is the check its own row asked for:
# with the published `  OK  http_pay == 200` mutated to `== 201` — the token
# swap this script cannot see — it exits 0, and the sentence it prints now names
# that swap in its NOT HELD clause. The message and the over-claim list can be
# read side by side without one contradicting the other, which is the property
# the old one-line OK did not have.
#
# K-1104 added three plants and a negative control: the « out of …» clause cut
# off a kept-side declaration, the «… are out» clause cut off another, and
# hoteling's issuer/payment-provider pair restored to its pre-K-1104 spelling
# («… only, ~60 lines apart in the Kiosk.configure block» — TRUE, and legal
# under the ruling since it does name the universe, but not in a shape a script
# can read, so it was reworded rather than exempted). All three red; a cut-side
# declaration lengthened without the word «only» stays green, which is the
# control that keeps the rule from being «the word `only` is banned».
#
# Stdlib only (ripper + json + RubyVM::AbstractSyntaxTree, the last only to ask a
# parser for one rake task's line range instead of guessing it from indentation),
# no bundle, no database, no Rails boot, well under a second on a bare checkout — the same profile as bin/check-demo-copies.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-demo-derivations             # check
#   bin/check-demo-derivations --list      # print every declared block
#   bin/check-demo-derivations --self-test # prove the promise rule on fixtures

require "json"
require "ripper"
require "set"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
DOC  = "before-after.md"

# The corpus, declared rather than discovered, for the same reason `ships:` is
# declared in bin/check-demo-copies: a demo that grows a narrative document, or
# drops one, changes the set with nothing noticing. prove has none — it is the
# API-only broker, it tells no storefront story.
DOCUMENTED_DEMOS = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling skooti].freeze

# The one shape a document's own comment may take inside a quoted snippet.
ELISION_MARKER = /\A\s*#\s*──\s+.+\s+ELIDED HERE \(this comment is the document's, not the file's\)\s+──\s*\z/

# What `strip_descriptions` leaves where a `description` call was. The four
# documents already use this exact line; it is here because the transformation
# has to be executable, and this is the executable half of it.
ELIDED_DESCRIPTION = 'description "…"   # elided — see the shipped file'

# Completeness promises. These are the sentences K-1062 and K-1063 found false;
# they are not banned outright (see the header — the repair wave's qualified
# forms are true and checkable), but a block that this script does not check may
# not carry one in the paragraph on either side of it.
PROMISES = [
  /nothing\s+else\s+(?:is\s+)?(?:touched|altered|left\s+out|removed|added)/i,
  /nothing\s+(?:is\s+)?removed\s+and\s+nothing\s+(?:is\s+)?added/i,
  /\bunedited\b/i,
  /\bverbatim\b/i,
  /first\s+line\s+to\s+last/i,
  /\bthe\s+\*?\*?whole\*?\*?\s+output\b/i,
  # The two the corpus taught us late (K-1106). «all three runs» is the
  # strongest completeness claim in these four documents and matched nothing;
  # «none of its chatter is in here» is the same claim wearing its negative
  # face, an assertion about what the block does NOT contain, which membership
  # cannot reach either. Both were added because a document said them, which is
  # the honest account of how the six above got here too — this list is a
  # VOCABULARY, not a classifier, and the over-claim list says so.
  /\ball\s+(?:\w+\s+)?runs\b/i,
  /\bnone\s+of\s+\S+[^.]{0,60}?\bis\s+in\s+here\b/i,
].freeze

# The two block classes the ban covers: `none`, checked by nothing, and
# `transcript`, checked by a SUBSET test. Hoisted out of the check below so the
# self-test can assert the set rather than re-type it (T-129).
PROMISE_KINDS = %w[none transcript].freeze

# THE SENTENCE THAT PUT EACH REGEX IN THE LIST — and it is a GATE, not a
# citation (T-129).
#
# `PROMISES` is a phrase BAN, and a ban that is being obeyed matches NOTHING.
# MEASURED at head over the four documents: SIX of the eight regexes have zero
# hits anywhere, which is the K-1106 repair wave's success rather than a defect.
# That makes the usual vacuity arm — «a pattern that matches nothing in this
# repository must fail» — exactly backwards here, and it leaves the rule with a
# hazard the other kind of rule does not have: a regex broken by an edit is
# INDISTINGUISHABLE from a regex nobody has violated, and the run stays green.
# It is the same shape as EARLIER_WIRES one repo over, which matched nothing for
# its entire existence while reading as coverage.
#
# So the proof is a POSITIVE CONTROL instead. Every regex carries the sentence
# that put it in the list — six of them the false sentences K-1062 and K-1063
# found, two of them the claims K-1106's re-measurement caught the list missing
# — and `--self-test` asserts each one still matches, and matches through the
# real neighbourhood window rather than on its own.
PROMISE_EXEMPLARS = [
  "every line is that file's with its `description` cut out and nothing else altered",
  "Nothing is removed and nothing is added.",
  "From there down it is unedited.",
  "The transcript below is verbatim.",
  "It is the run's own output, first line to last.",
  "This is the **whole** output of the task.",
  "The block carries all three runs.",
  "It is the task's own output and none of its chatter is in here.",
].freeze

TRANSFORMS = %w[strip_descriptions dedent].freeze

# ── `derived: generator` — a COMMAND, bound to the generator that answers it
#    (K-1099) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three of the four `derived: none` hatches were the SAME fenced block —
# `rails g kiosk:install`, byte-identical (md5 15a40864170e18899d5ea98c2fdf0632)
# in atablefor, getgrocery and hoteling — i.e. a CROSS-DEMO fact spelled as an
# exemption repeated once per document. Nothing would have noticed one of them
# drifting to `rails g kiosk:setup`, nor the generator being renamed under all
# three at once.
#
# Cross-demo agreement is the WEAK oracle here: three identical copies of a
# wrong command agree perfectly. The strong one is that `kiosk:install` is not a
# fact about the demos at all — it is the generator's own PATH. Rails resolves
# an invocation to `lib/generators/<ns>/<name>/<name>_generator.rb`, and derives
# the same namespace a second time from the class nesting inside that file
# (`Kiosk::Generators::InstallGenerator` → thor's `kiosk:generators:install_generator`
# → `_generator` stripped → `:generators:` collapsed → `kiosk:install`). So the
# block is checked against BOTH derivations, which must agree with each other
# and with every line of the block. Rename the directory, the file, or the
# class, and all three documents fail in the same run.
#
# It is STATIC — no Rails boot, no bundle, no database, the same profile as the
# rest of this script. Booting Rails to ask the real generator lookup would be
# the only stronger check and it is not affordable here; what the static pair
# cannot see is a generator that is found under this namespace but does
# something other than what the prose beside the block says it emits (that
# sentence is K-1069's territory, in bin/check-demo-copies).
#
# `ships:`-style corpus declaration, same reason as DOCUMENTED_DEMOS above: a
# demo that silently drops the adoption recipe changes the set with nothing
# noticing. skooti ships no recipe section — its document declares one block in
# total — so it is excluded BY DECLARATION, not by an exception.
GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS = %w[atablefor getgrocery hoteling].freeze

GENERATOR_PATH_RE = %r{(?:\A|/)lib/generators/(?<ns>[a-z0-9_]+)/(?<name>[a-z0-9_]+)/\k<name>_generator\.rb\z}

# The runner spellings an adopter may reasonably type. Deliberately a closed
# list: the point is that the NAMESPACE is derived, not that any command shape
# is waved through.
GENERATOR_RUNNERS = ["rails", "bin/rails", "bundle exec rails"].freeze
GENERATOR_VERBS   = %w[g generate].freeze

def generator_invocations(namespace)
  GENERATOR_RUNNERS.product(GENERATOR_VERBS).map { |runner, verb| "#{runner} #{verb} #{namespace}" }
end

# Rails::Generators::Base.namespace, reproduced from the source text rather than
# by loading it: thor's namespace of the class nesting, with a trailing
# `_generator` stripped and a `:generators:` segment collapsed.
def generator_namespace_from_source(body)
  mods  = body.scan(/^\s*module\s+([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*$/).flatten
  klass = body[/^\s*class\s+([A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*Generator)\s*<\s*(?:::)?Rails::Generators::(?:Base|NamedBase)\b/, 1]
  return nil if klass.nil?

  parts = (mods + [klass]).map { |c| c.gsub(/([a-z\d])([A-Z])/, '\1_\2').downcase }
  parts.join(":").sub(/_generator\z/, "").sub(/:generators:/, ":")
end

# The marker a `#{…}` interpolation leaves behind in a line pattern.
#
# IT USED TO BE A RAW NUL BYTE — `"\x00WILD\x00"` with the byte itself in the
# source — chosen because NUL is the one byte that cannot appear in the Markdown
# this guards. The byte made THIS FILE binary to grep: `command grep -n
# GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS bin/check-demo-derivations` printed one line, «Binary
# file … matches», and EXITED 0. A session grepping its own guard for a constant
# therefore got a success code and no evidence — and neither of the workspace's
# two standing greps-lie rules fires on it, because it reproduces under
# `/usr/bin/grep` and because the exit code is 0 with a REAL match (T-128).
#
# U+FFFF instead, written as an ASCII escape so this file is plain ASCII and
# stays greppable. It cannot collide with quoted content, for a reason STRONGER
# than NUL's was: U+FFFF is a permanent noncharacter — Unicode guarantees it is
# never assigned and it is not valid in interchange text — whereas NUL was
# reachable from an ordinary producer literal through `unescape`'s own `\0`
# mapping just below. And the reason is CHECKED rather than trusted:
# `line_patterns` aborts if an unescaped literal ever contains it, so a
# collision is a loud failure instead of a silently mis-split pattern.
WILD = "\uFFFF"

ESCAPES = { "n" => "\n", "t" => "\t", "s" => " ", "r" => "\r", "e" => "\e",
            "0" => "\0", "a" => "\a", "b" => "\b", "f" => "\f", "v" => "\v" }.freeze

def unescape(str)
  str.gsub(/\\(u\{[0-9a-fA-F ]+\}|u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|.)/m) do
    c = Regexp.last_match(1)
    if ESCAPES.key?(c)             then ESCAPES[c]
    elsif c.start_with?("u{")      then c[2..-2].split.map { |h| h.to_i(16) }.pack("U*")
    elsif c.start_with?("u")       then [c[1..].to_i(16)].pack("U")
    else                                c
    end
  end
end

# Every string literal in `src`, as line patterns with `#{…}` replaced by WILD.
# `src` is source TEXT rather than a path so that the caller keeps a label for it;
# the label is what the collision abort below names.
#
# ADJACENT LITERALS ARE MERGED. `"a" \` newline `"b"` is one Ruby string and is
# how nearly every long assertion message in these demos is written; Ripper
# reports it as two literals, and treating it as two would leave the check
# unable to see the very lines K-1062 recorded.
def line_patterns(src, label)
  toks = Ripper.lex(src)
  out  = []
  buf  = nil
  depth = 0
  gap = false

  flush = lambda do
    next if buf.nil?
    out.concat(buf.split("\n", -1))
    buf = nil
  end

  toks.each do |(_pos, type, tok, _state)|
    if depth.positive?
      case type
      when :on_embexpr_beg, :on_lbrace then depth += 1
      when :on_embexpr_end, :on_rbrace then depth -= 1
      end
      next
    end

    case type
    when :on_tstring_beg, :on_heredoc_beg, :on_words_beg, :on_qwords_beg, :on_regexp_beg
      if buf && gap
        gap = false          # adjacent literal: keep accumulating into `buf`
      else
        flush.call
        buf = +""
      end
    when :on_tstring_content
      next unless buf

      text = unescape(tok)
      if text.include?(WILD)
        abort "check-demo-derivations: a string literal in #{label} contains U+FFFF, the character " \
              "line patterns use to mark a `\#{}` interpolation. The marker must be one no producer " \
              "can emit; choose another rather than letting this literal be split at it."
      end
      buf << text
    when :on_embexpr_beg
      if buf
        buf << WILD
        depth = 1
      end
    when :on_tstring_end, :on_heredoc_end, :on_regexp_end
      gap = true if buf
    when :on_sp
      nil                    # spaces and `\`-continued newlines do not break adjacency
    else
      flush.call if gap
      gap = false
    end
  end
  flush.call
  out
end

# Key names a producer NAMES: `foo:` labels, `:foo` symbols and identifier-shaped
# string literals — the three ways a key is written where a JSON body is built.
# Deliberately NOT every `:on_ident`: a local variable is not a published field
# name, and admitting one would let any identifier anywhere in the producer
# legitimise any key.
def key_names(src)
  names = Set.new
  symbol = false
  Ripper.lex(src).each do |(_pos, type, tok, _state)|
    case type
    when :on_label then names << tok.chomp(":")
    when :on_symbeg then symbol = true
    when :on_ident, :on_const
      names << tok if symbol
      symbol = false
    when :on_tstring_content
      names << tok if tok.match?(/\A[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*\z/i)
      symbol = false
    else
      symbol = false if type != :on_sp
    end
  end
  names
end

# ── WHAT A WILDCARD IS ALLOWED TO HAVE CAPTURED (K-1105) ─────────────────────
#
# A frame's wildcard stands for a value the producer computed, and until K-1105
# any whitespace-free capture was waved through on the argument that ids, URLs
# and ports vary per run and cannot be predicted. True of those — and false of
# the two things the corpus's verdict lines actually interpolate, which is what
# made `  OK  http_pay == 500` and `  OK  http_paaaay == 200` acceptable. So a
# whitespace-free capture is now held to what its producers can SAY, in two
# vocabularies of different strength.

# A capture that is nothing but a number. `1_000` and a leading `+` are Ruby
# spellings a transcript could carry, so they are normalized rather than
# rejected; anything with a unit, a separator or a sign glued to other text
# (`27c`, `08:00`, `id=27`) is not a bare number and takes the token rule
# below instead.
NUMERIC_TOKEN = /\A[+-]?\d[\d_]*(?:\.\d+)?\z/

def normalize_number(tok)
  tok.sub(/\A\+/, "").delete("_")
end

# THE NUMBERS A PRODUCER NAMES — its own integer and float literals, and
# nothing else. Not the numbers inside its message strings, and this is the
# sharp end of the rule rather than an oversight: hoteling's demo.rake prints
# a red-team table containing the words «never a 500», so a pool built from
# string text would have named 500 and the mutation this rule exists to catch
# would have gone green (measured — it did, before this narrowing). A number a
# producer can only print as part of a fixed sentence needs no place here,
# because a line carrying it matches through that sentence's own literal.
def numeric_literals(src)
  nums = Set.new
  Ripper.lex(src).each do |(_pos, type, tok, _state)|
    nums << normalize_number(tok) if %i[on_int on_float].include?(type)
  end
  nums
end

# THE WORDS A PRODUCER CAN PRINT — every whitespace-free run inside a string
# literal, plus symbol names and numerals. Weaker than the numeric pool on
# purpose: this is a vocabulary, not a value set, and its job is only to
# refuse text the producer has no way to emit at all (`http_paaaay`,
# `cancelled`). Deliberately NOT identifiers and NOT comment words — `ok` is a
# local variable in three of these rake files and admitting locals would have
# legitimised `slots_badzone_code == ok`, measured.
def printable_tokens(src)
  toks = Set.new
  symbol = false
  Ripper.lex(src).each do |(_pos, type, tok, _state)|
    case type
    when :on_tstring_content then tok.split(/\s+/).each { |w| toks << w unless w.empty? }
    when :on_int, :on_float  then toks << normalize_number(tok)
    when :on_symbeg          then symbol = true
    when :on_ident, :on_const
      toks << tok if symbol
      symbol = false
    else
      symbol = false if type != :on_sp
    end
  end
  toks
end

# ── `abridged:`, bound to the rake DAG (K-1101) ──────────────────────────────

# A colon-joined lowercase chain — `demo:setup`, `db:schema:load`. That is how a
# rake task is spelled, and nothing else these declarations say is spelled that
# way: `description:` and `keys_from:` have a space after the colon, a URL has a
# slash, and a constant is capitalised.
ABRIDGED_TASK_RE = /(?<![\w:])[a-z][a-z0-9_]*(?::[a-z][a-z0-9_]*)+(?![\w:])/

# ── `abridged:` MEANS THE CUT, AND THE KEPT SENSE NEEDS A UNIVERSE (K-1104) ──
#
# The field's job is to describe what the reader CANNOT see, so the CUT sense
# is canonical. A phrase naming what was KEPT determines the complement just as
# exactly — but only when it also names the universe the kept lines came out
# of. «the two stripe lines only, out of the whole environment file» passes;
# «RUN 1 and the offline-token negatives» does not, and that was skooti's
# transcript for as long as the document existed.
#
# This pair of regexes is the mechanical half of that ruling and is a SHAPE
# check, not a truth check — see the over-claim list. A declaration that says
# «only» has committed to the kept sense out loud; it must then say what it was
# only-of.
KEPT_SIDE_RE = /(?<![\w-])only(?![\w-])/i
UNIVERSE_RE  = /(?<![\w-])out\s+of(?![\w-])|(?<![\w-])(?:are|is)\s+out(?![\w-])/i

# The two spellings a rake task is declared with. Kept as one predicate because
# the `task:` field and the `abridged:` field must answer "is this task this
# demo's?" the same way, and two copies of the pair would drift.
def task_defined_here?(rake_files, short)
  rake_files.any? { |rf| rake_task_declaration_in(File.read(rf), short) }
end

def rake_task_declaration_in(body, short)
  body.lines.each_with_index do |line, idx|
    next unless line.match?(/\A\s*task\s+:#{Regexp.escape(short)}\b/) ||
                line.match?(/\A\s*task\s+#{Regexp.escape(short)}:/)

    return [idx + 1, line]
  end
  nil
end

# [file, lineno, line] of the declaration, across the demo's rake files.
def rake_task_declaration(rake_files, short)
  rake_files.each do |rf|
    hit = rake_task_declaration_in(File.read(rf), short)
    return [rf, hit[0], hit[1]] if hit
  end
  nil
end

# What a task declares as its prerequisites, verbatim: `task x: :y do` and
# `task x: ["a:b", "c:d"]` both answer here, `task :x do` has none.
def rake_task_prereq_clause(decl_line)
  decl_line.to_s[/\A\s*task\s+[a-z0-9_]+:\s*(\S.*?)\s*(?:\bdo\b.*)?\z/, 1]
end

def prereq_mentions?(clause, full_name)
  return false if clause.nil?

  short = full_name.split(":").last
  clause.include?(full_name) || clause.match?(/(?<!\w)#{Regexp.escape(short)}(?!\w)/)
end

# The source text of a rake task's own body — the parser's line range, not an
# indentation guess. `nil` when the task is declared without a block
# (`task demo: [...]`), which prints nothing of its own.
def rake_task_body_source(rake_files, short)
  file, lineno, = rake_task_declaration(rake_files, short)
  return nil if file.nil?

  src = File.read(file)
  root = begin
    RubyVM::AbstractSyntaxTree.parse(src)
  rescue SyntaxError
    return nil
  end

  range = nil
  walk = lambda do |node|
    return unless node.is_a?(RubyVM::AbstractSyntaxTree::Node)

    if node.type == :ITER && node.first_lineno == lineno
      call = node.children.first
      if call.is_a?(RubyVM::AbstractSyntaxTree::Node) && call.children.include?(:task)
        range = [node.first_lineno, node.last_lineno]
      end
    end
    node.children.each { |c| walk.call(c) }
  end
  walk.call(root)
  return nil if range.nil?

  src.split("\n", -1)[(range[0] - 1)..(range[1] - 1)].join("\n")
end

# LITERAL TEXT AT ALL — the bar for a frame whose captures ARE checked, where
# anything non-empty is enough because the `.*?` cannot then swallow prose.
def anchor_strength(pattern)
  pattern.split(WILD, -1).sum { |part| part.gsub(/\s/, "").length }
end

# IDENTIFYING literal text — the bar for a frame matched WITHOUT reading its
# captures, where the literal is the only thing between this check and a
# rubber stamp. Punctuation is not identifying: it is the scaffolding every
# line of a given shape shares, so it says nothing about WHICH line this is.
#
# That distinction is K-1105, and the corpus supplies the counter-example.
# `puts "  OK  #{label} == #{expected}"` — the frame behind twenty of the
# twenty-six published `OK … == …` verdict lines, i.e. the lines that state
# whether the payment, the booking and the unlock worked — has four non-space
# literal characters, `OK` and `==`. It therefore cleared a bar of 4 on the
# UNCHECKED path and both of its wildcards went unread. Measured before the
# fix: hoteling's own `  OK  http_pay == 200` mutated to `  OK  http_pay ==
# 500 (charged nothing, refunded twice)` exited 0, and so did an invented
# `  OK  totally_made_up_verb == 999 utterly invented prose`.
#
# Counting only alphanumerics leaves that frame the anchor it always really
# had — `OK`, i.e. 2 — so it falls to the checked path, where the prose
# capture must resolve to a literal and does not.
#
# THE THRESHOLD IS UNCHANGED AT 4 BECAUSE THE METRIC WAS THE DEFECT, NOT THE
# NUMBER. Measured at head, every frame this corpus legitimately needs on the
# unchecked path names something — `Ordering:` 8, `Bearer` 6, `intent-` 6,
# `/kiosk/` 5, `Properties: … found, using property_id=… ` 33 — and the whole
# corpus stays green with punctuation discounted. (Raising the OLD metric
# instead was measured too: green to 9, red at 10, where `Ordering: #{list}`
# drops out. That is a number with nothing behind it but today's corpus.)
def anchor_identity(pattern)
  pattern.split(WILD, -1).sum { |part| part.gsub(/[^[:alnum:]]/, "").length }
end

def pattern_regex(pattern, capture:)
  parts = pattern.split(WILD, -1).map { |p| Regexp.escape(p) }
  glue  = capture ? "(.*?)" : ".*?"
  Regexp.new("\\A#{parts.join(glue)}\\z", Regexp::MULTILINE)
end

# How deep the frame composition may go. `puts "  OK    #{msg}"` with `msg`
# itself built as `"… (got #{x})"` is two levels, and that is the deepest shape
# these four demos use; the limit is what keeps a 500-pattern corpus from
# exploring its own cross product.
MAX_DEPTH = 3

class Patterns
  # `sources` and `key_sources` are `[label, source-text]` pairs.
  def initialize(sources, key_sources = [])
    pats   = sources.flat_map { |(label, src)| line_patterns(src, label) }.uniq
    @exact = Set.new(pats.reject { |p| p.include?(WILD) }.map(&:rstrip).reject(&:empty?))
    framed = pats.select { |p| p.include?(WILD) }
    # A frame matched WITHOUT checking its wildcards needs real literal text of
    # its own: `"#{a}#{b}"` matches everything and would make this a rubber
    # stamp. A frame whose wildcards ARE checked can be as thin as `"  OK  "` —
    # the capture has to resolve to a literal, so the `.*?` cannot swallow
    # arbitrary prose. That distinction is the whole reason there are two lists.
    #
    # The two lists therefore measure the anchor DIFFERENTLY, which is K-1105:
    # the unchecked list wants identifying text and punctuation is not it, while
    # the checked list only needs the frame to be a frame at all. The unchecked
    # list also keeps its literal SEGMENTS, so that a match can be held to the
    # one rule that survives not reading the captures — see
    # `no_capture_swallows_its_terminator?`.
    @loose = framed.select { |p| anchor_identity(p) >= 4 }.map { |p| [pattern_regex(p, capture: true), p.split(WILD, -1)] }
    @tight = framed.select { |p| anchor_strength(p) >= 1 }.map { |p| pattern_regex(p, capture: true) }
    @keys  = (sources + key_sources).flat_map { |(_label, src)| key_names(src).to_a }.to_set
    # THE TWO CAPTURE VOCABULARIES (K-1105). `key_sources` are excluded from both
    # for the same reason their literals never join the line patterns: a handler
    # is consulted for field NAMES only, and folding a large file's constants in
    # would legitimise any number and any word at all.
    @nums = sources.flat_map { |(_label, src)| numeric_literals(src).to_a }.to_set
    @toks = sources.flat_map { |(_label, src)| printable_tokens(src).to_a }.to_set
    @memo  = {}
  end

  def key?(name) = @keys.include?(name)

  def accounts_for?(line, depth = 0)
    line = line.rstrip
    return true if @exact.include?(line)
    return false if depth >= MAX_DEPTH

    key = [line, depth]
    return @memo[key] if @memo.key?(key)

    @memo[key] = false # cut recursion on a cycle
    ok = @loose.any? { |(re, parts)| (m = re.match(line)) && no_capture_swallows_its_terminator?(m, parts) } ||
         @tight.any? do |re|
           m = re.match(line)
           m && m.captures.all? { |c| capture_ok?(c, depth) }
         end
    @memo[key] = ok
  end

  # THE ONE RULE THAT SURVIVES NOT READING THE CAPTURES: a frame's literal
  # scaffolding must appear where the frame says it does. `.*?` is lazy, but
  # backtracking will still make it eat a literal it was supposed to stop at
  # when that is the only way to reach `\z` — so hoteling's
  # `  OK  bookings.confirmation_code == the code returned (#{code})` matched
  # its own published line with ` (charged nothing, refunded twice)` appended,
  # the last of the fifty published-line mutations to stay green. Requiring
  # each capture not to contain the literal that terminates it is what a reader
  # already assumes lazy matching means, and it costs the corpus nothing.
  def no_capture_swallows_its_terminator?(match, parts)
    match.captures.each_with_index.none? do |cap, i|
      lit = parts[i + 1].to_s
      !lit.empty? && cap.to_s.include?(lit)
    end
  end

  # A wildcard's captured text stands on its own when it is empty, or a single
  # whitespace-free token that is not a bare number (an id, a URL, a path, a
  # port, a code). Anything with a space in it is prose, and prose must come
  # from a literal.
  #
  # A BARE NUMBER IS THE ONE TOKEN CLASS HELD TO THE PRODUCER (K-1105). Every
  # other whitespace-free token genuinely varies per run — a UUID, a `pi_…`
  # reference, a timestamp, a port — and pinning those is what design fact 3
  # refuses. A number in a verdict line is not that: it IS the result, and
  # `  OK  http_pay == 500` says the payment failed. Held to the numeric
  # literals of the declared producers, that line fails — hoteling's five
  # producers name 21 distinct integers and 500 is not among them — where the
  # published `== 200` passes. This is the only rule in the transcript check
  # that reaches a VALUE rather than a shape, and it reaches exactly as far as
  # the producers' own numerals; the over-claim list says what that leaves.
  #
  # Any OTHER whitespace-free capture is held to the weaker `@toks` vocabulary
  # — it must at least be a word one of the declared producers can print. A
  # UUID, a `pi_…` reference or a port is not in it and would fail, which is
  # why this rule only ever fires on a frame thin enough to reach the checked
  # path: every frame in this corpus that interpolates a run-varying id names
  # enough around it to be matched whole on the unchecked path instead.
  def capture_ok?(text, depth)
    t = text.strip
    return true if t.empty?

    if t !~ /\s/
      return @nums.include?(normalize_number(t)) if t.match?(NUMERIC_TOKEN)

      return @toks.include?(t)
    end

    accounts_for?(t, depth + 1)
  end
end

# ── The declared, EXECUTABLE transformations ─────────────────────────────────

# Index just past the end of a (possibly `\`-continued) string literal starting
# at or after `lines[li][ci]`, plus whatever follows it on that final line.
def scan_string_value(lines, li, ci)
  i = li
  col = ci
  loop do
    s = lines[i] or return [li, ""]
    q = s.index(/["']/, col) or return [i, ""]
    quote = s[q]
    j = q + 1
    j += 1 while j < s.length && !(s[j] == quote && s[j - 1] != "\\")
    tail = s[(j + 1)..] || ""
    return [i, tail] unless tail.rstrip.end_with?("\\")

    i += 1
    col = 0
  end
end

def strip_descriptions(src)
  lines = src.split("\n", -1)
  out = []
  i = 0
  while i < lines.length
    line = lines[i]

    if (m = line.match(/\A(\s*)description\s+["']/))
      last, = scan_string_value(lines, i, m[1].length + "description".length)
      out << "#{m[1]}#{ELIDED_DESCRIPTION}"
      i = last + 1
      next
    end

    if (k = line.index(/(?<![\w:])description:\s/))
      pre = line[0...k]
      last, tail = scan_string_value(lines, i, k + "description:".length)
      tail = tail.sub(/\A\s*,/, ",")
      if pre.strip.empty?
        out << (out.pop || "").rstrip.sub(/,\z/, "") + tail
      else
        out << pre.rstrip.sub(/,\z/, "") + tail
      end
      i = last + 1
      next
    end

    out << line
    i += 1
  end
  out
end

def transformed_lines(src, name)
  case name
  when "strip_descriptions" then strip_descriptions(src)
  when "dedent"             then src.split("\n", -1).map(&:lstrip)
  else raise ArgumentError, name
  end
end

# ── Document parsing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Block = Struct.new(:fence_line, :info, :body, :decl, :decl_line, :neighbourhood,
                   :preceding_paragraph, keyword_init: true)

DECL_RE = /\A<!--\s*derived:\s*(.*?)\s*-->\z/

def parse_declaration(text)
  fields = {}
  text.split("|").each_with_index do |part, idx|
    if idx.zero?
      fields["derived"] = part.strip
      next
    end
    k, v = part.split(":", 2)
    next if v.nil?

    fields[k.strip] = v.strip
  end
  fields
end

def parse_document(path)
  lines = File.readlines(path, chomp: true)
  blocks = []
  prev_after = nil
  i = 0
  while i < lines.length
    unless lines[i].start_with?("```")
      i += 1
      next
    end

    info = lines[i][3..].to_s.strip
    j = i + 1
    j += 1 while j < lines.length && !lines[j].start_with?("```")
    decl_line = nil
    decl = nil
    if i.positive? && (m = DECL_RE.match(lines[i - 1].strip))
      decl_line = i - 1
      decl = parse_declaration(m[1])
    end
    blocks << Block.new(
      fence_line: i + 1, info: info, body: lines[(i + 1)...j],
      decl: decl, decl_line: decl_line && decl_line + 1,
      neighbourhood: [promise_window_before(lines, decl_line || i, prev_after),
                      paragraph_after(lines, j)].join("\n"),
      preceding_paragraph: paragraph_before(lines, decl_line || i),
    )
    prev_after = paragraph_after_range(lines, j)
    i = j + 1
  end
  blocks
end

# The ONE paragraph on each side of a block — the prose that introduces it and
# the prose that comments on it. Deliberately not "everything since the previous
# block": the gap between two blocks belongs to both of them, so a wider window
# attributes one document's sentence to a block it was never about.
def paragraph_before_range(lines, idx)
  k = idx - 1
  k -= 1 while k >= 0 && lines[k].strip.empty?
  stop = k
  k -= 1 while k >= 0 && !lines[k].strip.empty?
  (k + 1)..stop
end

def paragraph_before(lines, idx)
  lines[paragraph_before_range(lines, idx)].to_a.join("\n")
end

def paragraph_after_range(lines, idx)
  k = idx + 1
  k += 1 while k < lines.length && lines[k].strip.empty?
  start = k
  k += 1 while k < lines.length && !lines[k].strip.empty? && !lines[k].start_with?("```")
  start...k
end

def paragraph_after(lines, idx)
  lines[paragraph_after_range(lines, idx)].to_a.join("\n")
end

# ── What «beside» means for the PROMISE rule, and why it is WIDER (K-1106) ───
#
# `paragraph_before` above takes ONE paragraph, and getgrocery's «unedited,
# first line to last» sits TWO above its declaration, behind «**This recording
# is the secret-free path**». So the promise rule scored ZERO on the document
# whose promise is as unbacked as any other in the corpus.
#
# The promise window is therefore the INTRO: every line from the nearest
# preceding heading, horizontal rule or fenced block down to the declaration.
# That is deliberately the wide window `paragraph_before`'s own comment argues
# against — and the argument does not reach this rule. It is about a check that
# READS the prose as a description of THIS block, where attributing one block's
# sentence to another makes the check answer about the wrong thing. This rule
# reads nothing: it asks only whether an unbackable sentence stands near a block
# nothing can back it for. The worst a mis-attribution can do here is demand
# that a sentence be qualified, and that demand is right wherever the sentence
# sits. MEASURED at head: the wider window adds `verbatim` beside all three
# `derived: generator` blocks, reaching back over the Gemfile snippet's
# commentary; the rule does not fire on `generator`, so it costs nothing today.
# AND THE FLOOR, WHICH IS THE PRICE THE WIDE WINDOW CAME WITH (T-129).
#
# The nearest boundary above a declaration is, where two blocks share a section
# with no heading between them, the EARLIER block's closing fence — so the wide
# window reaches back over that block's own trailing commentary and attributes
# its sentences to this one. MEASURED at head before this floor existed, over
# all 25 declared blocks: `verbatim` was inherited that way by SEVEN blocks, all
# of them from a preceding Gemfile or routes commentary.
#
# T-129 filed that as latent, on the reading that the rule fires only on
# `transcript` and `none` and none of those inherited anything. RE-MEASURED
# 2026-08-31, that reading is WRONG IN ONE PLACE and the row is corrected here
# rather than carried: the four transcript blocks are each the first declared
# block under a heading and their windows are clean, but hoteling's ONE
# `derived: none` block sits directly below a `snippet`, and its window DOES
# span that snippet's commentary. Nothing fires today only because those eight
# lines happen to contain no promise word — and «nothing else altered» beside a
# snippet is the QUALIFIED form the K-1062 repair wave deliberately wrote, so
# the next author to write one there reddens a block it was never about.
#
# THE FLOOR, and the exception is the whole design. The window starts after the
# previous block's after-paragraph — UNLESS that paragraph is also this block's
# own `paragraph_before`, i.e. the gap holds exactly ONE paragraph and it serves
# both. hoteling's does: «Swapping in real payments is one line and touches no
# other code:» ends in a colon that introduces the very block below it while
# also commenting on the one above. Flooring unconditionally would delete that
# block's real intro from its window and take the rule's only `none` subject
# with it. So a shared single paragraph stays attributed to both — which is
# right, because a reader cannot tell either, and that ambiguity is a fair thing
# to be asked to fix by adding a heading or a rule.
def promise_window_boundary?(line)
  line.start_with?("```") || line.start_with?("#") || line.strip.match?(/\A-{3,}\z/)
end

def promise_window_before(lines, idx, prev_after = nil)
  k = idx - 1
  k -= 1 while k >= 0 && !promise_window_boundary?(lines[k])
  floor = k + 1
  if prev_after && prev_after.end > prev_after.begin &&
     paragraph_before_range(lines, idx).begin != prev_after.begin
    floor = [floor, prev_after.end].max
  end
  lines[floor...idx].to_a.join("\n")
end

# ── A NUMBERED CUT LIST, AND THE NAMES IN IT (K-1107) ────────────────────────
#
# `abridged:` is the half of the pair this script reads; the paragraph three
# lines above it is the half a reader reads. In two of K-1103's five instances
# the PARAGRAPH WAS RIGHT WHILE THE FIELD WAS WRONG — the worse direction,
# because the field is the half with a checker. So when that paragraph
# enumerates the cut as NUMBERED sentences, every backticked bare identifier in
# those sentences must appear in the field.
#
# SENTENCE-SCOPED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. Measured rather than argued:
# the naive whole-paragraph form false-positives on THREE of the corpus's five
# enumerating paragraphs, because each one ends in a KEPT clause — «Everything
# else — field names, types, `required` lists, `enum`s …» — whose backticks name
# what STAYED. Scoping to the `(N)` sentences leaves exactly one live hit at
# head, and that hit is real.

# A backticked span that is a bare identifier: `kyc_status`, `description`,
# `description:`. Deliberately NOT `description "…"   # elided` or `BODY
# ELIDED` — those are prose, and a field is not expected to echo them verbatim.
BARE_IDENTIFIER_RE = /\A[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:?\z/

# Sentences, backtick-aware, because the corpus writes `db:schema:load` and
# `Date.today + 30` mid-sentence and a `.` inside backticks ends nothing.
def sentences(text)
  flat = text.tr("\n", " ")
  out = []
  buf = +""
  in_code = false
  flat.each_char.with_index do |c, i|
    buf << c
    in_code = !in_code if c == "`"
    next unless c == "." && !in_code

    nxt = flat[i + 1]
    next unless nxt.nil? || nxt.match?(/\s/)

    out << buf.strip
    buf = +""
  end
  out << buf.strip
  out.reject(&:empty?)
end

# The `(N)` sentences of a paragraph that enumerates a cut. TWO are required:
# a single stray `(1)` is a parenthesis, not an enumeration.
def enumerated_cut_items(paragraph)
  items = sentences(paragraph).select { |s| s.match?(/\A\(\d+\)\s/) }
  items.length >= 2 ? items : []
end

def backticked_identifiers(text)
  text.scan(/`([^`]+)`/).flatten.select { |s| s.match?(BARE_IDENTIFIER_RE) }.uniq
end

# How many limits WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER actually lists, COUNTED from this
# file rather than remembered. The success line points a CI reader at that list
# (K-1108), and a pointer carrying a stale number is a defect a sibling
# inventory gate has already shipped: it announced «6 work-list(s)» where its
# code implemented five, for days, at exit 0. It aborts rather than
# print a zero, because a pointer that has lost its target is a false one.
def over_claim_limit_count
  section = File.read(__FILE__)[/^# .. WHAT THIS DOES \*\*NOT\*\* COVER.*?^# EACH CLASS ABOVE WAS PROVEN/m]
  n = section.to_s.scan(/^#   \* /).size
  abort "check-demo-derivations: cannot find its own WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER list — the success " \
        "message points a CI reader at it, so the pointer may not go stale silently." if n.zero?

  n
end

# ── --self-test: the promise rule proves itself on fixtures (T-129) ─────────
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AND WHY IT IS SCOPED THE WAY IT IS. Everything else in this
# script is proven by a plant in the real corpus, and the header records twelve
# of them. The promise rule cannot be: it is a phrase BAN whose live subject set
# is EMPTY by design (six of the eight regexes match nothing anywhere at head,
# because K-1106's repair wave removed the sentences), and its window is a
# geometry that only bites where two blocks share a section — a shape the four
# documents contain in exactly one place today. Both are invisible to a bare
# run. So this mode carries fixtures: eight positive controls for the
# vocabulary, and four documents built to have the geometry the corpus barely
# has.
#
# IT DOES NOT COVER THE REST OF THE SCRIPT. The membership, JSON, elision,
# `abridged:` and generator rules are proven by planting in the real corpus, and
# a `--self-test` that claimed to cover them would be the over-claim this file's
# own header list exists to prevent.
def self_test
  require "tmpdir"
  failures = []
  arms = 0
  check = lambda do |name, ok, detail = nil|
    arms += 1
    failures << "#{name}#{detail ? " — #{detail}" : ""}" unless ok
  end

  # A. VOCABULARY, one positive control per regex. A ban that matches nothing is
  #    indistinguishable from a ban nobody is violating, so each pattern is held
  #    to the sentence that put it in the list.
  unless PROMISES.length == PROMISE_EXEMPLARS.length
    failures << "PROMISES has #{PROMISES.length} regex(es) and PROMISE_EXEMPLARS " \
                "#{PROMISE_EXEMPLARS.length} sentence(s) — every pattern needs the sentence that " \
                "put it in the list, or the next one added is proven by nothing"
  end
  PROMISES.each_with_index do |re, i|
    exemplar = PROMISE_EXEMPLARS[i].to_s
    check.("vocabulary[#{i}] #{re.inspect} matches its exemplar", exemplar =~ re,
           "#{exemplar.inspect} no longer matches — the pattern was edited and nothing else can " \
           "tell you, because a ban with no live subject stays green")
  end

  # B. THE KINDS. The ban covers the two classes nothing can back a completeness
  #    claim for, and deliberately not `snippet`, whose qualified form IS backed.
  check.("PROMISE_KINDS is exactly none+transcript", PROMISE_KINDS.sort == %w[none transcript],
         "got #{PROMISE_KINDS.inspect} — widening this set is the change T-129 is about, and it " \
         "may not happen without re-measuring the window's false positives")

  # C. THE WINDOW. Four fixture documents, each one block class over the same
  #    geometry, run through the REAL parse_document.
  doc = lambda do |body|
    Dir.mktmpdir do |dir|
      path = File.join(dir, "before-after.md")
      File.write(path, body)
      blocks = parse_document(path)
      last = blocks.last
      hit = PROMISE_KINDS.include?(last.decl["derived"]) && PROMISES.any? { |re| last.neighbourhood =~ re }
      [hit, last]
    end
  end

  shared_gap = <<~MD
    ## One section, two blocks, ONE paragraph between them

    <!-- derived: snippet | from: config/initializers/kiosk.rb -->
    ```ruby
      c.issuer = "https://example.test"
    ```

    Quoted verbatim from the initializer, and swapping the provider is one line:

    <!-- derived: none | why: an illustrative swap -->
    ```ruby
      c.payment_provider = Other.new
    ```
  MD
  check.("a promise in a gap of ONE paragraph still reaches the block below it", doc.(shared_gap).first,
         "the floor swallowed a paragraph that is the later block's own intro — hoteling's real " \
         "`derived: none` block has exactly this shape and it is the rule's only live subject")

  split_gap = <<~MD
    ## One section, two blocks, TWO paragraphs between them

    <!-- derived: snippet | from: config/initializers/kiosk.rb -->
    ```ruby
      c.issuer = "https://example.test"
    ```

    The two lines above are quoted verbatim from the initializer.

    Here is the swap an operator would make:

    <!-- derived: none | why: an illustrative swap -->
    ```ruby
      c.payment_provider = Other.new
    ```
  MD
  check.("a promise in the EARLIER block's own commentary does not reach the block below it",
         !doc.(split_gap).first,
         "the unfloored window reaches back over a snippet's commentary and reddens a `none` " \
         "block for a sentence that was never about it — the T-129 false positive")

  own_intro = <<~MD
    ## A block whose OWN intro promises

    <!-- derived: transcript | from: demo:thing | abridged: nothing -->
    ```text
      OK  thing
    ```

    The block above is unedited.
  MD
  check.("a promise in the block's OWN commentary still fires", doc.(own_intro).first,
         "the rule stopped seeing the paragraph directly after a block, which is where four of " \
         "the corpus's eleven promises sat")

  snippet_ok = <<~MD
    ## A snippet may carry its qualified sentence

    <!-- derived: snippet | from: config/routes.rb -->
    ```ruby
      root "home#index"
    ```

    Every line is that file's with its `description` cut out and nothing else altered.
  MD
  check.("a snippet's qualified promise is NOT banned", !snippet_ok.then { |b| doc.(b).first },
         "the ban reached `snippet`, whose form is backed by the declared strip_descriptions " \
         "transform — sweeping it in is the over-claim K-1106 declined")

  if failures.empty?
    puts "check-demo-derivations --self-test: OK — #{arms} arm(s): #{PROMISES.length} vocabulary " \
         "positive control(s), the covered-kind set, and 4 fixture document(s) for the promise " \
         "window (shared gap, split gap, own commentary, snippet control)."
    exit 0
  end
  warn "\ncheck-demo-derivations --self-test FAILED (#{failures.length} of #{arms} arm(s)):\n"
  failures.each { |f| warn "  ✗ #{f}" }
  warn "\nThese arms are the ONLY proof the promise rule still fires: its regexes are a ban whose " \
       "live subject set is empty by design, so a broken one leaves a bare run green."
  exit 1
end

self_test if ARGV.include?("--self-test")

# ── Checks ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

errors = []
listing = ARGV.include?("--list")
counts = Hash.new(0)
generator_blocks = Hash.new(0)

present = Dir.glob(File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-*", DOC)).map { |p| File.basename(File.dirname(p)).sub("kiosk-demo-", "") }.sort
undeclared = present - DOCUMENTED_DEMOS
missing = DOCUMENTED_DEMOS - present
undeclared.each do |demo|
  errors << "check-demo-derivations: kiosk-demo-#{demo}/#{DOC} exists but #{demo} is not in " \
            "DOCUMENTED_DEMOS. A demo that grows a narrative document joins the corpus by being " \
            "declared, never by being discovered — the same reason `ships:` is declared in " \
            "bin/check-demo-copies."
end
missing.each do |demo|
  errors << "check-demo-derivations: #{demo} is in DOCUMENTED_DEMOS but ships no #{DOC}. Delete the " \
            "entry or restore the document; a corpus that silently shrinks checks nothing."
end

(DOCUMENTED_DEMOS & present).each do |demo|
  dir = File.join(ROOT, "kiosk-demo-#{demo}")
  doc = File.join(dir, DOC)
  label = "kiosk-demo-#{demo}/#{DOC}"
  rake_files = Dir.glob(File.join(dir, "lib/tasks/*.rake"))

  parse_document(doc).each do |block|
    where = "#{label}:#{block.fence_line}"

    if block.decl.nil?
      errors << "#{where}: fenced block with no `<!-- derived: … -->` declaration on the line above " \
                "it. Every block in a narrative document must name its producer machine-readably " \
                "(`transcript`, `snippet`) or be declared `none` with a reason — a block nobody " \
                "declared is a block nobody checks, which is how ~30 defects reached these four " \
                "documents (K-1051)."
      next
    end

    kind = block.decl["derived"]
    unknown = block.decl.keys - %w[derived task from keys_from transform abridged why]
    unless unknown.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: unknown key(s) #{unknown.join(', ')} in the derivation declaration."
    end

    # The promise rule covers BOTH block classes whose checker cannot back a
    # completeness claim: `none`, checked by nothing, and `transcript`, checked
    # by a SUBSET test. It used to sit inside the `none` branch below — i.e.
    # beside exactly ONE block — hoteling's `derived: none` swap, the one
    # declared block this script does not check — and beside none of the four
    # that quote a recorded run, which is where every falsehood K-1062 and
    # K-1063 found had been. See the K-1106 header section.
    if PROMISE_KINDS.include?(kind) && (hit = PROMISES.find { |re| block.neighbourhood =~ re })
      why_unbacked = if kind == "none"
                       "the block is declared `derived: none`, so nothing checks it at all"
                     else
                       "a transcript is checked by MEMBERSHIP, which backs «every line here is a " \
                       "line the task prints» and can never back a completeness claim — K-1062's " \
                       "hoteling block was missing an ENTIRE RUN the task prints and every line " \
                       "it did quote was genuine"
                     end
      errors << "#{where}: the prose beside this block promises completeness " \
                "(#{block.neighbourhood[hit].inspect}) while #{why_unbacked}. Say what the " \
                "mechanism DOES hold and leave completeness to the `abridged:` field, which is a " \
                "human's signature and says so — or drop the sentence (K-1106)."
    end

    if kind == "none"
      if block.decl["why"].to_s.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: none` needs a `why:` — an undeclared exemption is the one " \
                  "thing this check cannot see through, so it must at least be argued for."
      end
      counts[:none] += 1
      puts "  none       #{where}  (#{block.decl['why']})" if listing
      next
    end

    # A COMMAND an adopter types is not output this repo produces, which is why
    # these three blocks sat under `derived: none` — but the command's namespace
    # IS produced here, by the generator that answers it. See the header block
    # at GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS for why cross-demo agreement is the weaker oracle.
    if kind == "generator"
      generator_blocks[demo] += 1
      rel = block.decl["from"].to_s.strip
      if rel.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: generator` needs a `from:` naming the generator that answers " \
                  "the invocation. It is REPO-relative, unlike a transcript's or a snippet's — this " \
                  "producer lives in the engine gem rather than in the demo, which is the whole " \
                  "point of the binding."
        next
      end
      src = File.join(ROOT, rel)
      unless File.file?(src)
        errors << "#{where}: `from: #{rel}` names no file in this repository. The block quotes an " \
                  "invocation with nothing to answer it."
        next
      end
      m = GENERATOR_PATH_RE.match(rel)
      if m.nil?
        errors << "#{where}: `from: #{rel}` is not a Rails generator path " \
                  "(`…/lib/generators/<ns>/<name>/<name>_generator.rb`), so no namespace can be " \
                  "derived from it and there is nothing to hold the invocation to."
        next
      end
      namespace  = "#{m[:ns]}:#{m[:name]}"
      from_class = generator_namespace_from_source(File.read(src))
      if from_class.nil?
        errors << "#{where}: #{rel} defines no `class …Generator < Rails::Generators::Base` " \
                  "(or NamedBase), so it is not a generator and cannot answer `#{namespace}`."
        next
      end
      if from_class != namespace
        errors << "#{where}: #{rel} derives `#{namespace}` from its PATH but `#{from_class}` from " \
                  "its class nesting. Rails resolves an invocation by both; while they disagree, " \
                  "one of the two is what an adopter typing this block's command actually gets."
        next
      end
      accepted = generator_invocations(namespace)
      block.body.each_with_index do |line, k|
        next if line.strip.empty?

        lineno = block.fence_line + 1 + k
        next if accepted.include?(line.strip.gsub(/\s+/, " "))

        errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: this line does not invoke `#{namespace}`, the namespace " \
                  "#{rel} answers — accepted spellings are #{accepted.join(', ')}:\n      " \
                  "#{line.strip[0, 160]}"
      end
      counts[:generator] += 1
      puts "  generator  #{where}  #{namespace}  <- #{rel}" if listing
      next
    end

    unless %w[transcript snippet].include?(kind)
      errors << "#{where}: `derived: #{kind}` is not one of transcript, snippet, generator, none."
      next
    end

    froms = block.decl["from"].to_s.split(",").map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?)
    if froms.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: `derived: #{kind}` needs a `from:` naming the producer file(s), " \
                "demo-relative."
      next
    end
    keys_from = block.decl["keys_from"].to_s.split(",").map(&:strip).reject(&:empty?)
    if !keys_from.empty? && kind != "transcript"
      errors << "#{where}: `keys_from:` is a transcript-only declaration — it names the handlers " \
                "whose field names appear in the driver's JSON summary line."
      next
    end
    absent = (froms + keys_from).reject { |f| File.file?(File.join(dir, f)) }
    unless absent.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: `from:`/`keys_from:` names #{absent.join(', ')}, which kiosk-demo-#{demo} " \
                "does not ship. A producer that moved leaves the block quoting nothing."
      next
    end

    abridged = block.decl["abridged"].to_s
    if abridged.empty?
      errors << "#{where}: `abridged:` is required. Say what was CUT — that is the field's job, " \
                "because the cut is the part a reader cannot see; `none` when nothing was. A " \
                "phrase naming what was KEPT is admissible only when it ALSO names the universe " \
                "those lines came out of («the two stripe lines only, out of the whole " \
                "environment file»), because that and only that lets a reader compute the " \
                "complement (K-1104). Abridgement must be DECLARED in a form this check reads; " \
                "every prose promise of the «nothing else touched» kind in these four documents " \
                "was false (K-1062/K-1063)."
      next
    end

    if abridged.match?(KEPT_SIDE_RE) && !abridged.match?(UNIVERSE_RE)
      errors << "#{where}: `abridged: #{abridged}` says what was KEPT — it says «only» — without " \
                "naming the universe those lines were kept OUT OF, so a reader cannot compute " \
                "what was cut, which is the field's one job. Either state the cut directly (the " \
                "canonical sense) or add the universe: «… only, out of X», «… only; Y are out» " \
                "(K-1104). This is a SHAPE check and knows nothing about whether the universe " \
                "named is the right one."
    end

    # The paragraph above a declaration and the declaration itself describe the
    # same cut, and only one of the two has ever had a checker. When the
    # paragraph enumerates the cut in numbered sentences, the two are held
    # together BY NAME (K-1107).
    cut_items = enumerated_cut_items(block.preceding_paragraph.to_s)
    unless cut_items.empty?
      unnamed = backticked_identifiers(cut_items.join(" "))
                .reject { |id| abridged.match?(/(?<![A-Za-z0-9_])#{Regexp.escape(id)}/) }
      unless unnamed.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: the paragraph above this block enumerates the cut and names " \
                  "#{unnamed.join(', ')}, which `abridged: #{abridged}` does not. Both describe " \
                  "the same cut and this script reads only the declaration, so a field that omits " \
                  "what the prose enumerates leaves the CHECKED half saying less than the " \
                  "unchecked one — which is the direction two of K-1103's five instances ran " \
                  "(K-1107). NAME-OVERLAP only: it cannot tell whether either half is right."
      end
    end

    if kind == "transcript"
      task = block.decl["task"].to_s
      if task.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: transcript` needs a `task:` naming the command that printed it."
        next
      end
      name = task.split.last.to_s
      short = name.split(":").last
      unless task_defined_here?(rake_files, short)
        errors << "#{where}: `task: #{task}` names `#{name}`, which no lib/tasks/*.rake in " \
                  "kiosk-demo-#{demo} defines. The block claims a producer that does not exist."
        next
      end

      # A task NAMED in `abridged:` is resolved against the rake DAG and held off
      # the block. See the header section on K-1101 for what this does and does
      # not buy — the cut's CONTENT stays the human's word.
      broken_abridgement = false
      abridged.scan(ABRIDGED_TASK_RE).uniq.each do |cited|
        cited_short = cited.split(":").last
        unless task_defined_here?(rake_files, cited_short)
          errors << "#{where}: `abridged:` cites `#{cited}`, which no lib/tasks/*.rake in " \
                    "kiosk-demo-#{demo} defines. Name the TASK whose output was cut, not the " \
                    "commands it happens to run — this field is the human's signature on a " \
                    "completeness claim nothing else can check, so it may only cite something " \
                    "this script can resolve (K-1101)."
          broken_abridgement = true
          next
        end

        clause = rake_task_prereq_clause(rake_task_declaration(rake_files, short)&.last)
        unless prereq_mentions?(clause, cited)
          errors << "#{where}: `abridged:` cites `#{cited}`, but `#{name}` does not declare it as " \
                    "a prerequisite#{clause ? " (its prerequisites are `#{clause}`)" : ' — it declares none'}. " \
                    "A task the quoted one never runs contributed nothing to its output, so its " \
                    "absence is not an abridgement; `abridged: none` is what a separately-prepared " \
                    "database calls for."
          broken_abridgement = true
          next
        end

        frag = rake_task_body_source(rake_files, cited_short)
        next if frag.nil?

        cut_pats = Patterns.new([["#{cited}'s own body", frag]])
        block.body.each_with_index do |line, k|
          next if line.strip.empty?
          next unless cut_pats.accounts_for?(line)

          errors << "#{label}:#{block.fence_line + 1 + k}: this line is printed by `#{cited}`, whose " \
                    "output the declaration says was CUT. The declaration and the block disagree:\n" \
                    "      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
          broken_abridgement = true
        end
      end
      next if broken_abridgement

      pats = Patterns.new(froms.map { |f| [f, File.read(File.join(dir, f))] },
                          keys_from.map { |f| [f, File.read(File.join(dir, f))] })
      block.body.each_with_index do |line, k|
        next if line.strip.empty?

        lineno = block.fence_line + 1 + k

        # A JSON object line goes through the KEY rule and nowhere else. It used
        # to fall through to the literal rule first, and a thin frame elsewhere
        # in the producer would match the whole line — a planted key rename went
        # green that way, which is the exact defect this rule exists to catch.
        obj = if line.strip.start_with?("{") && line.strip.end_with?("}")
                begin
                  JSON.parse(line.strip)
                rescue JSON::ParserError
                  nil
                end
              end

        if obj.is_a?(Hash)
          unknown_keys = []
          walk = lambda do |node|
            case node
            when Hash then node.each { |kk, vv| unknown_keys << kk unless pats.key?(kk); walk.call(vv) }
            when Array then node.each { |vv| walk.call(vv) }
            end
          end
          walk.call(obj)
          if unknown_keys.empty?
            counts[:json] += 1
            next
          end
          errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: the JSON summary line carries key(s) " \
                    "#{unknown_keys.uniq.join(', ')} that no file in `from:` or " \
                    "`keys_from:` names. A published summary naming a field the driver stopped " \
                    "emitting is K-1053 exactly; a nested key belongs to the handler that " \
                    "serialises it, so name that handler in `keys_from:`."
          next
        end

        next if pats.accounts_for?(line)

        errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: this line is not a line `#{task}` prints — no string " \
                  "literal in #{froms.join(', ')} produces it:\n      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
      end
      counts[:transcript] += 1
      puts "  transcript #{where}  #{task}  <- #{froms.join(' ')}" if listing
    else
      src_path = File.join(dir, froms.first)
      if froms.length > 1
        errors << "#{where}: `derived: snippet` takes exactly one `from:` — a snippet quotes ONE " \
                  "file, and a union of files is not a thing a reader can go and check."
        next
      end

      transforms = block.decl["transform"].to_s.split(",").map(&:strip).reject { |t| t.empty? || t == "none" }
      bad = transforms - TRANSFORMS
      unless bad.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: transform(s) #{bad.join(', ')} are not implemented here. The declared " \
                  "transformation has to be EXECUTABLE; #{TRANSFORMS.join(', ')} are."
        next
      end

      if abridged == "none" && !transforms.empty?
        errors << "#{where}: `abridged: none` beside `transform: #{transforms.join(', ')}`. A " \
                  "transformation IS an abridgement — say what it takes out."
      end

      src = File.read(src_path)
      raw_lines = Set.new(src.split("\n", -1).map(&:rstrip))
      allowed = raw_lines.dup
      transforms.each { |t| transformed_lines(src, t).each { |l| allowed << l.rstrip } }
      lstripped = Set.new(allowed.map(&:lstrip)) if transforms.include?("dedent")
      needed_transform = false

      # The first line may be the conventional `# <path>` banner. It is prose,
      # not a line of the file — but it must name the file the declaration does.
      body = block.body
      if (m = body.first.to_s.match(%r{\A#\s*([A-Za-z0-9_./-]+)}))
        if m[1] == froms.first
          body = body[1..]
        else
          errors << "#{where}: the block's opening banner names #{m[1]} while the declaration says " \
                    "`from: #{froms.first}`. One of the two is wrong and a reader cannot tell which."
          body = body[1..]
        end
      end

      in_note = false
      body.each_with_index do |line, k|
        lineno = block.fence_line + (block.body.length - body.length) + 1 + k
        if line =~ ELISION_MARKER
          if abridged == "none"
            errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: a marked elision inside a block declared " \
                      "`abridged: none`. The declaration and the block disagree."
          end
          in_note = true
          counts[:elision] += 1
          next
        end
        if in_note
          next if line.strip.start_with?("#")

          in_note = false
        end
        next if line.strip.empty?
        if allowed.include?(line.rstrip) || lstripped&.include?(line.lstrip.rstrip)
          needed_transform ||= !raw_lines.include?(line.rstrip)
          next
        end

        if line.strip.start_with?("#") && !src.include?(line.strip)
          errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: a comment line that is not in #{froms.first} and is not " \
                    "introduced by the marked-elision line. A document comment a reader cannot " \
                    "tell from shipped code is the whole reason that marker exists:\n" \
                    "      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
          next
        end

        errors << "#{label}:#{lineno}: this line is in no version of #{froms.first} " \
                  "(raw#{transforms.empty? ? '' : " or #{transforms.join('/')}"}):\n" \
                  "      #{line.strip[0, 160]}"
      end
      if !transforms.empty? && !needed_transform
        errors << "#{where}: `transform: #{transforms.join(', ')}` is declared but no line in the " \
                  "block needs it — every quoted line is already in the raw file. A transformation " \
                  "is declared because it was APPLIED; a decorative one is a prose promise again."
      end
      counts[:snippet] += 1
      puts "  snippet    #{where}  #{froms.first}#{transforms.empty? ? '' : "  [#{transforms.join(' ')}]"}" if listing
    end
  end
end

# The adoption recipe is a CROSS-DEMO section, so its presence is declared, not
# discovered — a demo that drops it, or grows one nobody declared, changes the
# set with nothing noticing (K-1099, and the same argument as DOCUMENTED_DEMOS).
(DOCUMENTED_DEMOS & present).each do |demo|
  want = GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS.include?(demo) ? 1 : 0
  got  = generator_blocks[demo]
  next if got == want

  errors << if want == 1
              "kiosk-demo-#{demo}/#{DOC}: declares #{got} `derived: generator` block(s), expected 1. " \
              "#{demo} is in GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS, so its adoption recipe must show the generator " \
              "invocation and that invocation must be bound to the generator that answers it."
            else
              "kiosk-demo-#{demo}/#{DOC}: declares #{got} `derived: generator` block(s) but #{demo} " \
              "is not in GENERATOR_BLOCK_DEMOS. A new copy of a cross-demo command may not appear " \
              "silently — declare it there."
            end
end

if counts[:transcript].zero? && counts[:snippet].zero? && counts[:generator].zero? && errors.empty?
  errors << "check-demo-derivations: found NO declared blocks at all. A rule that inspects nothing " \
            "passes everything."
end

# The success line carries its own SCOPE, because the header's over-claim list
# is the only place that scope used to live and no CI log carries a header
# (K-1108). The sentence this replaces — «every line held to a literal its
# declared producer prints» — is the exact sentence that printed while hoteling's
# published `  OK  http_pay == 200` had been mutated to `== 500 (charged
# nothing, refunded twice)` and this script exited 0. A green run must say what
# it did NOT check where the reader is, not where the author is.
if errors.empty?
  puts "check-demo-derivations: OK — #{counts[:transcript]} transcript, #{counts[:snippet]} " \
       "snippet and #{counts[:generator]} generator-invocation block(s) across " \
       "#{(DOCUMENTED_DEMOS & present).size} narrative documents."
  puts "  HELD: every line is a literal one of its declared producers prints (a frame is read " \
       "through its captures unless four alphanumerics of its own identify the line), a line of " \
       "the file it quotes, or the namespace the named generator answers; #{counts[:json]} JSON " \
       "summary line(s) key-checked; #{counts[:elision]} marked elision(s); #{counts[:none]} " \
       "block(s) declared underived with a reason; no completeness promise beside a block " \
       "nothing can back one for."
  puts "  NOT HELD: JSON VALUES, a SWAP between two tokens the producers both know " \
       "(`== 200` -> `== 201` passes), line ORDER, single-run provenance, and COMPLETENESS — " \
       "that no line is missing is the `abridged:` field's claim, signed by a human, and this " \
       "script only keeps it consistent with the rake DAG and the block. Every limit this script " \
       "knows of is listed under WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER at the top of the file " \
       "(#{over_claim_limit_count} of them); read those before quoting this line."
  exit 0
end

warn "\ncheck-demo-derivations FAILED:\n"
errors.each { |e| warn "  ✗ #{e}" }
warn "\nEvery fenced block in a kiosk-demo-*/before-after.md must declare its producer in an " \
     "`<!-- derived: … -->` comment, and every line in it must be a line that producer prints or " \
     "a line of the file it quotes. Abridgement is DECLARED, never promised in prose."
exit 1
